MiniShield — Day 2: the step-by-step teaching guide
Recorded from the study session on 2026-11-12

How to use this guide
====================

The learner writes the implementation. These notes are updated as lessons
and code reviews happen in the conversation, including errors, explanations,
and checks. Exercises and proposed next steps are distinguished from work
that has been verified. Each exercise introduces one change,
explains the types and behavior involved, and supplies a check before the
next step. This guide continues day1, starting with PHI demotion.


1. Collect PHI nodes before modifying them
=========================================

The flattening pass currently checks eligibility without changing IR. The
first exercise is to collect pointers to PHI instructions in an eligible
function. Do this after the eligibility check succeeds.

Include llvm/IR/Instructions.h, llvm/Support/Casting.h, and <vector>.
Declare a collection of pointers to the instructions we intend to demote:

  std::vector<llvm::PHINode *> phis;

Traverse the function using mutable references:

  for (auto &BB : F) {
      for (auto &I : BB) {
          // Identify PHIs and collect their pointers.
      }
  }

F is a mutable Function reference, so BB refers to an existing mutable
BasicBlock and I refers to an existing mutable Instruction. Using const
references would give pointers to const instructions, unsuitable for the
later modifying operation.

For each instruction, dyn_cast<llvm::PHINode>(&I) checks whether it is a
PHI. Store the returned pointer and append it only when non-null:

  if (auto *phi = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::PHINode>(&I)) {
      phis.push_back(phi);
  }

After both loops, print phis.size(). Keep returning
PreservedAnalyses::all() at this stage because collection changes no IR.

Rebuild and run:

  cmake --build build

  opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
    -passes='function(minishield-flatten),verify' \
    -disable-output tests/ir/phi.ll

Expected PHI counts:

  phi.ll                1
  loop.ll               1
  minishield-input.ll   0

These are collection checks, not evidence that demotion has occurred.


2. Diagnose the push_back pointer-type mismatch
==============================================

The learner initially wrote:

  if (llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::PHINode>(&I)) {
      phis.push_back(&I);
  }

The condition correctly checks whether the instruction is a PHI, but the
push_back argument still has the wrong C++ type.

Read the types separately:

  I                    llvm::Instruction &
  &I                   llvm::Instruction *
  phis element type    llvm::PHINode *

PHINode derives from Instruction. A PHINode pointer can be converted to an
Instruction pointer, but an arbitrary Instruction pointer cannot implicitly
be converted to a PHINode pointer. It might refer to an add, branch, return,
or another instruction type.

Calling dyn_cast in a condition does not change the declared type of I or
the type of the expression &I. C++ does not narrow that expression merely
because the check succeeded. In the first version, the useful PHINode
pointer returned by dyn_cast was discarded.

Keep that returned pointer instead:

  if (auto *phi = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::PHINode>(&I)) {
      phis.push_back(phi);
  }

Now phi has type llvm::PHINode *, exactly matching the vector's element
type. The if condition tests that pointer: non-null enters the body;
nullptr skips it. The variable phi is available inside the if body.

No instruction is copied or newly allocated. I and *phi refer to the same
underlying instruction; phi provides access through its more specific PHI
type. Appending phi copies the pointer into the vector, not the instruction.

Checkpoint: explain why phis.push_back(&I) fails, why phis.push_back(phi)
succeeds, and why a successful type check does not retype the original
variable. Distinguish the instruction object from a pointer used to access it.


3. Next exercise: demote after collection finishes
=================================================

After the collection checks pass, the next step is to include
llvm/Transforms/Utils/Local.h and invoke llvm::DemotePHIToStack(phi) for each
collected pointer. This is a free function taking one PHI pointer, not a
DemotePHIToStack::runOnFunction(F) method.

Collect first and transform afterward. DemotePHIToStack deletes the PHI it
processes; modifying the instruction list during ordinary traversal can
invalidate the iterator. Do not dereference a collected PHI pointer after
its node has been demoted.

Once IR changes, return PreservedAnalyses::none() unless specific analyses
are explicitly maintained. If there are no PHIs and nothing changes, return
PreservedAnalyses::all(). The implementation and verification of that
transformation are the next exercise, not completed work in these notes.


4. Find values used outside their defining block
===============================================

After PHI demotion works, inspect the demoted loop fixture. A PHI becomes
memory operations, but the result of a load can still be used across blocks:

  header:
    %i.reload = load i32, ptr %i.reg2mem

In loop.ll, i.reload is also used in body and exit. Currently header
dominates those uses. A future dispatcher could introduce paths to those
blocks that bypass header, breaking that dominance relationship.

Lesson: removing PHIs does not remove all cross-block SSA dependencies.
Before building the dispatcher, identify the remaining values that escape
their defining blocks. The exercise is to collect them, not yet transform
them.

After PHI demotion, declare:

  std::vector<llvm::Instruction *> escapingValues;

Traverse the function again using mutable block and instruction references.
Skip instructions with void result type and AllocaInst instructions for this
exercise. Then collect instructions for which this expression is true:

  I.isUsedOutsideOfBlock(&BB)

Print each collected instruction's name. For the current demoted loop.ll,
expect i.reload. The stack allocation is deliberately excluded; keeping
stack storage in the true entry block is part of the dispatcher design.
This temporary exclusion is not a general proof that every possible alloca
is safe to move or flatten.

Do not return early just because phis.empty(). Functions without PHIs can
still contain cross-block values that need inspection. Record whether PHI
demotion changed the IR, for example:

  bool changed = !phis.empty();

At the end, return PreservedAnalyses::none() if IR changed; otherwise return
PreservedAnalyses::all(). Merely collecting escapingValues changes nothing.
Once later transformations modify IR, include those changes in the decision.

Checkpoint: identify the definition of i.reload and its uses in other blocks.
Explain why a dispatcher can invalidate dominance even after PHIs disappear.


5. Check an instruction's result type for void
============================================

The learner asked how to identify instructions that do not produce values.
Use:

  I.getType()->isVoidTy()

I.getType() returns a pointer to the instruction's result Type. isVoidTy()
tests whether that result type is void. Inside the instruction loop:

  if (I.getType()->isVoidTy())
      continue;

continue advances to the next iteration of the inner instruction loop.
It does not leave the function or skip all remaining blocks.

Compare these result types:

  %x = add i32 %a, %b          i32
  %p = alloca i32             ptr
  store i32 %x, ptr %p        void
  br label %next             void
  ret i32 %x                 void

The last example is important. ret i32 %x returns a value from the function,
but the ret instruction itself produces no value for another instruction
to use. Its result type is void. Do not confuse an instruction's result
type with an operand's type or the enclosing function's return type.

Checkpoint: explain why a store and a return are skipped, while an integer
addition can be a candidate for cross-block value demotion.


6. Recognize alloca and choose isa versus dyn_cast
================================================

The learner next asked how to recognize AllocaInst. Use LLVM's type test:

  if (llvm::isa<llvm::AllocaInst>(I))
      continue;

An alloca instruction produces a pointer to allocated stack storage. Its
result type is ptr, not void, so the void check alone does not exclude it.

Combine the two filters when collecting escaping values:

  if (I.getType()->isVoidTy() ||
      llvm::isa<llvm::AllocaInst>(I))
      continue;

Then apply isUsedOutsideOfBlock(&BB) to the remaining instructions and
append their addresses to escapingValues when the test succeeds.

Choose the LLVM casting helper according to what the code needs:

  isa<T>(I)
    Returns a boolean: is this instruction a T?
    Appropriate here because the filter only needs to recognize alloca.

  dyn_cast<T>(&I)
    Returns a pointer to T when the type matches, or nullptr otherwise.
    Appropriate in PHI collection because the vector needs a PHINode pointer.

Include llvm/IR/Instructions.h for the instruction classes and
llvm/Support/Casting.h for the casting helpers. The learner already added
these during the PHI collection exercise.

Checkpoint: explain why alloca survives the void-result filter, why isa is
sufficient to skip it, and why PHI collection instead kept dyn_cast's result.

The next exercise is to review escapingValues, then use DemoteRegToStack
to replace suitable cross-block instruction values with memory accesses.
That transformation has not been implemented by these teaching notes.


7. Review the collection loop and remove the premature return
===========================================================

At the next code review, the void/alloca filter was present, but the loop
did not yet collect anything after the filter. Declaring escapingValues
and skipping unsuitable instructions does not append the remaining values.

Immediately after the filter, add:

  if (I.isUsedOutsideOfBlock(&BB)) {
      escapingValues.push_back(&I);
  }

Unlike the earlier PHI vector, this vector stores Instruction pointers.
Here &I already has exactly the required type, llvm::Instruction *. No
downcast is necessary. The PHI vector needed the PHINode pointer returned
by dyn_cast because its element type was more specific.

The review also found the earlier unchanged-case return still present:

  if (phis.empty())
      return llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();

Remove that early return when adding cross-block processing. A function
without PHIs can still have an instruction value used in another block,
so this return would bypass the new stage entirely.

Record whether PHI demotion changes IR before processing the PHI vector:

  bool changed = !phis.empty();

The PHI loop naturally does nothing when the vector is empty. Continue
into escaping-value collection for every eligible function. At the end,
while this new stage only collects values, return:

  return changed ? llvm::PreservedAnalyses::none()
                 : llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();

Print the collected names to inspect the result:

  for (const auto *value : escapingValues) {
      llvm::errs() << "cross-block value: " << value->getName() << "\n";
  }

For the existing loop.ll after PHI demotion, expect i.reload. These name
diagnostics are a learning aid: LLVM instructions are not required to have
names, so future automated checks should not rely solely on them.

Checkpoint: explain why &I is correct for escapingValues.push_back but
was not correct for phis.push_back. Verify the collection before adding
another mutation step.


8. Preview the next transformation: demote escaping instruction values
=====================================================================

Once the completed collection has been checked, iterate the collected
pointers after traversal and use the utility already declared by
llvm/Transforms/Utils/Local.h:

  llvm::DemoteRegToStack(*value);

The first parameter is Instruction &, whereas DemotePHIToStack takes a
PHINode *. Dereference the stored Instruction pointer with *value to pass
the existing instruction by reference. This does not copy the instruction.

Record that IR changed whenever a value is demoted. The final preservation
decision must cover both PHI demotion and escaping-value demotion. Do not
modify the instruction list inside the collection loop.

This remains the next exercise. The current code review identified an
incomplete collection loop; it did not establish that this second demotion
stage had been implemented or tested.


9. Demote the collected cross-block values
=========================================

The next review found the collection completed: the premature PHI-empty
return was removed, void and alloca instructions were filtered, and
isUsedOutsideOfBlock(&BB) guarded escapingValues.push_back(&I). The pass
still only demoted PHIs; collecting the other values had not transformed
them yet.

Next exercise: after escaping-value collection has finished, add:

  for (auto *value : escapingValues) {
      llvm::DemoteRegToStack(*value);
  }

The vector stores Instruction pointers. This utility accepts an Instruction
reference, so *value accesses the existing instruction that the pointer
identifies. The Local.h header is already included.

The utility creates stack storage and stores the instruction's result,
then replaces uses with loads. This prepares cross-block data flow for
later CFG rewriting. It does not construct the dispatcher or rewrite the
original branch structure.

Update the changed decision to account for both transformations:

  bool changed = !phis.empty() || !escapingValues.empty();

Return PreservedAnalyses::none() when changed is true, and all() otherwise.
Checking only the PHI vector would incorrectly preserve analyses for a
function with no PHIs but with cross-block instruction values.

Rebuild and inspect the demoted loop fixture:

  cmake --build build

  opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
    -passes='function(minishield-flatten),verify' \
    -S tests/ir/loop.ll -o build/loop-demoted.ll

  cat build/loop-demoted.ll

Expect no PHI instructions and additional stack operations. The value
previously named i.reload should no longer be used directly from body or
exit; uses obtain the value through loads. Names and exact load counts
are implementation details, so inspect the data flow rather than matching
an invented exact output. Verification must succeed.

Also write tests/ir/cross-block.ll to exercise the case without any PHIs:

  define i32 @cross_block(i32 %x) {
  entry:
    %sum = add i32 %x, 1
    br label %exit
  exit:
    ret i32 %sum
  }

The sum is defined in entry but used in exit. Even though the PHI count is
zero, this value should be demoted and changed must be true. The intended
result stores sum into a stack slot and returns a load from that slot in
exit. This fixture guards against the earlier premature-return bug.

Checkpoint: explain why the second utility needs *value, why both vectors
affect invalidation, and why zero PHIs does not imply unchanged IR. The
second demotion stage and new fixture remain learner implementation work
until they have been written and checked.


10. Explain what demoting a collected value actually does
========================================================

The learner asked what demoting the collected values means. Distinguish
the C++ collection from the IR it identifies: escapingValues is a vector
of pointers to instructions. DemoteRegToStack rewrites the LLVM IR for each
pointed-to instruction; it does not turn the C++ vector into stack storage.

Start with this function:

  define i32 @cross_block(i32 %x) {
  entry:
    %sum = add i32 %x, 1
    br label %exit
  exit:
    ret i32 %sum
  }

The instruction in entry defines sum, but exit directly uses that SSA
value. Demoting sum conceptually produces the following IR; exact generated
names may differ:

  define i32 @cross_block(i32 %x) {
  entry:
    %sum.slot = alloca i32
    %sum = add i32 %x, 1
    store i32 %sum, ptr %sum.slot
    br label %exit
  exit:
    %sum.loaded = load i32, ptr %sum.slot
    ret i32 %sum.loaded
  }

The calculation remains. Its result is stored into a stack slot, and the
consumer reads that slot with a load instead of directly referencing the
original SSA result from another block. The stack-slot pointer still crosses
blocks; its allocation must remain in an entry block that dominates its uses.

This is why the step helps prepare for flattening. Rewriting control flow
through a dispatcher may break dominance for values originally defined in
one block and used in another. Memory provides a way to carry those values
along the intended execution path without that direct SSA use across blocks.
The dispatcher must still preserve execution order so stores happen before
the corresponding loads. Demotion alone does not prove a future dispatcher
correct.

Here register means an LLVM SSA, register-like value. It does not promise
that the original value occupied a particular physical CPU register. The
resulting alloca/load/store representation can be optimized again later.

Checkpoint: identify what stays unchanged (the addition), what is added
(the slot, store, and load), and why ret now uses sum.loaded instead of sum.


11. Compare PHI demotion with ordinary value demotion
===================================================

The learner next asked how PHI demotion differs. A PHI chooses its result
according to the edge used to enter its block, rather than performing an
ordinary calculation such as addition. In the diamond fixture:

  join:
    %value = phi i32 [ 10, %left ], [ 20, %right ]
    ret i32 %value

Entering join from left selects 10. Entering from right selects 20. A PHI
is not a runtime conditional instruction written as an if statement; it
expresses an SSA merge whose choice follows the incoming control-flow edge.

DemotePHIToStack replaces that merge with memory operations. For this simple
fixture, the conceptual transformed function is:

  define i32 @merge(i1 %condition) {
  entry:
    %value.slot = alloca i32
    br i1 %condition, label %left, label %right
  left:
    store i32 10, ptr %value.slot
    br label %join
  right:
    store i32 20, ptr %value.slot
    br label %join
  join:
    %value.loaded = load i32, ptr %value.slot
    ret i32 %value.loaded
  }

Exact generated names may differ. The slot is allocated in entry. Incoming
paths store their selected values before the merge block reads the slot.
The utility replaces PHI uses with loads and deletes the original PHI.
Here there is one return use and one load; more complicated use patterns
may produce different load placement or counts.

Compare the two transformations:

  DemoteRegToStack(*value)
    The ordinary value-producing instruction remains, for example add.
    Store its computed result and replace its uses with loads.

  DemotePHIToStack(phi)
    The PHI is removed. Stores on incoming paths represent its selection,
    and loads supply the selected value to its former users.

Both use memory to carry values, but the stored values come from different
places: an ordinary instruction's computed result versus a PHI's incoming
values selected by control flow. Function eligibility must already have
passed, and the eventual dispatcher must preserve the intended path order.

Checkpoint: trace condition=true through left and condition=false through
right. Identify which store executes and what join loads on each path.
Explain why only removing PHIs can still leave ordinary cross-block SSA
values, such as the i.reload result found in the loop fixture.


12. Verify demotion and assign dispatcher state IDs
==================================================

The follow-up review found both transformation loops implemented, with
changed accounting for both collections. The plugin was rebuilt, and
minishield-flatten followed by verify succeeded on tests/ir/loop.ll.

The generated IR showed two stack slots: one for the PHI and one for the
cross-block load result. header stores i.reload into the second slot;
body and exit each read that slot instead of directly using the header's
instruction result. No PHI remained. This confirms the observed structure
and verifier result, not a proof of behavior for every possible input IR.

Next exercise: assign integer state IDs without changing control flow yet.
Keep the existing function entry block as a one-time setup block. Its
allocations and initialization must not run again on every dispatcher
iteration. The dispatcher will route execution among the other blocks.

The intended shape is:

  original entry/setup -> dispatcher -> selected original block
                              ^                  |
                              +------------------+

Return and unreachable blocks terminate instead of looping back. The
dispatcher switch and terminator rewriting will be later steps; assigning
IDs alone must not change the CFG.

After demotion, introduce these containers in FlatteningPass::run():

  std::vector<llvm::BasicBlock *> stateBlocks;
  llvm::DenseMap<llvm::BasicBlock *, unsigned> stateIDs;

Include llvm/ADT/DenseMap.h. Obtain the actual entry block with
&F.getEntryBlock(), then iterate the function in its existing block order.
Skip that entry block. For every remaining block, assign the next ID
starting at zero, save its pointer in stateBlocks, and record the mapping
in stateIDs. Use pointer identity rather than block names.

Print mappings by iterating stateBlocks, not by iterating DenseMap. The
vector preserves the selected block order; DenseMap iteration order is
not a stable presentation order.

For phi.ll, expect:

  state 0 -> left
  state 1 -> right
  state 2 -> join

For loop.ll, expect:

  state 0 -> header
  state 1 -> body
  state 2 -> exit

The entry block gets no state in this design. The eventual entry terminator
must select the correct successor state; do not assume every entry always
starts state zero. A conditional entry can choose different states.

Checkpoint: explain why setup executes once, why lookup keys are pointers,
why the vector is used to print IDs, and why assigning IDs changes no IR.
State IDs, the dispatcher, and terminator rewriting remain learner exercises
until they have been implemented and checked.


13. Review pointer keys and DenseMap insertion
=============================================

The first state-mapping draft mixed a BasicBlock reference with a pointer
in three places. In for (auto &BB : F), BB is a reference to a block.
entry_ptr is a BasicBlock pointer, and both containers store block pointers.

Compare pointer identity using the block's address:

  if (entry_ptr == &BB)
      continue;

Append the same address to the pointer vector:

  stateBlocks.push_back(&BB);

Record the ID using the pointer as the map key:

  stateIDs[&BB] = id;

Alternatively, insert a key/value pair with braces:

  stateIDs.insert({&BB, id});

The draft stateIDs.insert(<BB, id>) was not valid C++ pair construction.
Angle brackets are used for template arguments in declarations such as
DenseMap<BasicBlock *, unsigned>; braces construct the pair passed to insert.
Choose one insertion form, then increment id once for each non-entry block.

When printing, the vector elements are already pointers:

  for (auto *BB : stateBlocks) {
      llvm::errs() << "state " << stateIDs.lookup(BB)
                   << " -> " << BB->getName() << "\n";
  }

Use getName() rather than streaming the pointer when a block label is the
intended output. Copying the vector's pointer element does not copy the
BasicBlock, so a reference variable is unnecessary in this loop.

Keep lookup pointers compatible with the map's BasicBlock * key type.
Writing const auto *BB would instead make BB a pointer to const BasicBlock,
which does not implicitly convert to the map's mutable pointer key type.
The original const auto BB form makes the copied pointer itself const,
not the pointed-to block; it is different from const auto *BB.

Checkpoint: explain why &BB is needed while iterating F, but BB is already
the correct key while iterating stateBlocks. Rebuild and confirm the three
expected state mappings before adding any dispatcher blocks.


14. Construct a complete but unconnected dispatcher
=================================================

The corrected state mapping was reviewed and run on phi.ll. It printed
left=0, right=1, join=2 and passed verification. Next, build the dispatcher
without connecting the original control flow to it yet.

Include llvm/IR/IRBuilder.h. Keep stateBlocks as the snapshot collected
before adding any new blocks; the dispatcher and invalid-state block must
not receive ordinary state IDs.

Move the existing changed declaration before the new construction code.
For functions with nonempty stateBlocks, create an i32 stack slot in the
original entry block:

  llvm::IRBuilder<> setup(&*entry_ptr->getFirstInsertionPt());
  auto *stateSlot = setup.CreateAlloca(setup.getInt32Ty(), nullptr, "ms.state");

getFirstInsertionPt() supplies an iterator. Dereference it to obtain an
instruction, then take its address to give the builder an insertion point.
The new allocation executes once because the original entry remains setup.

Append blocks named ms.dispatch and ms.invalid using BasicBlock::Create,
passing F.getContext(), the block name, and &F. Do not insert either before
the actual entry. A block created this way starts empty and must receive a
terminator before verification.

Use a builder for ms.invalid and call CreateUnreachable(). Use a builder
for ms.dispatch to create an i32 load from stateSlot, then a switch whose
condition is that load and whose default destination is ms.invalid:

  auto *current = dispatchBuilder.CreateLoad(
      dispatchBuilder.getInt32Ty(), stateSlot, "ms.current");
  auto *dispatchSwitch = dispatchBuilder.CreateSwitch(
      current, invalidBlock, stateBlocks.size());

The last argument reserves space for cases; it does not create them.
For every original state block, add its mapping:

  dispatchSwitch->addCase(
      dispatchBuilder.getInt32(stateIDs.lookup(BB)), BB);

Record changed=true whenever the new slot and blocks are created. Preserve
the existing conditional return of none() or all(); do not redeclare changed
at the end in a way that discards this new mutation. Skip dispatcher creation
when stateBlocks is empty.

Keep the original terminators unchanged in this stage. The dispatcher is
unreachable from the original entry, so its state load is not executed.
There is deliberately no initial state store yet: the entry terminator must
later choose the state corresponding to its actual successor, not blindly
assume state zero.

Rebuild, run minishield-flatten followed by verify on phi.ll, and inspect
the text output. Expect the state slot, a dispatcher load and switch with
cases 0->left, 1->right, 2->join, and an invalid block ending in unreachable.
Original branches still form the original diamond. This is a construction
checkpoint, not a completed flattening transformation.


15. Review BasicBlock creation: C++ variables versus LLVM names
=============================================================

The first dispatcher draft failed compilation. It declared objects using
ms.dispatch and ms.invalid as though dotted LLVM block names were C++
variable names, then attempted to take their addresses.

A dot is a member-access operator in C++, not a character allowed inside a
variable identifier. An LLVM block's display name is a string argument to
the creation function. BasicBlock::Create returns a BasicBlock pointer;
store that pointer directly rather than constructing a BasicBlock value:

  auto *dispatcher = llvm::BasicBlock::Create(
      F.getContext(), "ms.dispatch", &F);
  auto *invalidBlock = llvm::BasicBlock::Create(
      F.getContext(), "ms.invalid", &F);

Here dispatcher and invalidBlock are C++ pointer variables. ms.dispatch
and ms.invalid are string names attached to LLVM IR blocks. The function
owns the inserted blocks. No separate &ms.dispatch or &ms.invalid variables
are needed, and the placeholder name argument must be replaced by actual
string literals.

The draft also left the invalid block empty. Before running the verifier,
give it a terminator:

  llvm::IRBuilder<> invalidBuilder(invalidBlock);
  invalidBuilder.CreateUnreachable();

The switch already terminates the dispatcher block. The invalid block
requires its own unreachable terminator; a switch pointing to the block
does not terminate that destination block.

Construct the dispatcher builder directly from its pointer:

  llvm::IRBuilder<> dispatchBuilder(dispatcher);

The draft &*dispatcher expression reduces to the same pointer, but is
unnecessary here. Contrast it with &*entry_ptr->getFirstInsertionPt():
that expression starts from an iterator, so dereferencing and taking an
address is how the earlier example obtains an Instruction pointer.

The state slot, switch cases, and changed=true logic were present in the
draft. Fix the creation statements and invalid-block terminator, rebuild,
then run the verifier. The failed build did not produce a new plugin, so
running an older existing shared library would not validate this draft.

Checkpoint: distinguish a C++ identifier, a BasicBlock pointer, and an
LLVM block name string. Explain why every new block needs its own terminator.


16. Verify the dispatcher and connect an unconditional entry
==========================================================

The corrected construction compiled. Running minishield-flatten followed
by verify on phi.ll showed an entry state allocation, an ms.dispatch switch
with states 0->left, 1->right, 2->join, and ms.invalid ending in unreachable.
The dispatcher had no predecessors, as intended for the construction stage.

Next exercise: connect only an unconditional entry branch first. Use loop.ll,
whose original entry branches to header. Its header state is zero. Leave
conditional entry branches and other entry terminators unchanged for now.

Place the rewriting code inside the existing nonempty-stateBlocks branch,
after constructing the dispatcher, while stateSlot and dispatcher remain
in scope. Save the original entry terminator pointer, then construct an
IRBuilder using that instruction as the insertion point.

Switch on the saved terminator's opcode. For Instruction::UncondBr, obtain
its successor with getSuccessor(0), look up that block's state ID, create an
i32 constant with the builder's getInt32(), and emit a store to stateSlot.
Then emit a branch to dispatcher and erase the saved old terminator with
eraseFromParent(). End the case with break. The default case does nothing
at this stage, leaving unsupported-yet entry forms as they were.

The intended entry ending in loop.ll becomes:

  store i32 0, ptr %ms.state
  br label %ms.dispatch

Create the replacement instructions before deleting the old branch. The
old successor is needed to choose the state, and the old instruction also
provides the builder's insertion point. Do not use the erased pointer after
deletion, and never leave the block with both old and new terminators.

Rebuild, then run the pass followed by verify on tests/ir/loop.ll and inspect
the generated text. Expect ms.dispatch to acquire entry as a predecessor.
The other original blocks still branch directly to each other: only the
first hop now passes through the dispatcher. This is not full flattening.
The existing changed=true from dispatcher construction already covers this
additional mutation.

Checkpoint: explain why storing the successor ID must precede the branch,
why zero is correct for this fixture but not a universal initializer, and
why conditional entries require a separate successor-selection step next.
